=== coop/design-corrections/native/native-evidence.schemas.v2.json#/$defs/SourceUnitOwnershipV1
{
 "type": "object",
 "additionalProperties": false,
 "required": [
  "schemaVersion",
  "enumeration",
  "units",
  "selectedUnitIds",
  "ownership"
 ],
 "description": "The committed relation from a SNAPSHOT SOURCE PATH to the compilation TARGET that reads it, the closed table of those targets, and the EXPLICIT SELECTION of which of them this universe analyses. It exists because a body span dialect is a property of the compilation target: neither a crate-name-to-root map nor the package edition map can supply it, since #[path], files shared by several targets and several compilation contexts of one crate defeat a directory inference, and Cargo lets a target override its package default edition. Because this record is committed by H identity inside RustUniverseV2ResolvedInputs.sourceUnitOwnershipId, a different SELECTION is a different record, a different identity and therefore a DIFFERENT sourceUniverse: one physical source path compiled by two targets at two editions is validly analysed under each, one selection at a time, with no change to any relation payload. Every row is a retained native observation; nothing here executes Cargo, a build script or a repository read, and nothing here qualifies a compiler or an enumerator.",
 "properties": {
  "schemaVersion": {
   "const": 1
  },
  "enumeration": {
   "type": "string",
   "enum": [
    "complete",
    "partial"
   ],
   "description": "complete: every compiled source path OF THE SELECTED UNITS has a row, so a path with no row is not compiled by any selected target. partial: the producer did not finish, so an owner INSIDE the selected scope may exist that is not listed. These are different claims and the second is not a weaker form of the first: an unlisted owner could contradict a listed one, so no clone body dialect is admissible under partial. That is checked BEFORE any row lookup, so partial discovery can never act as an implicit edition selection by hiding a conflicting owner. Partial evidence remains useful under the existing facts and Coverage law: the clones scope reports its incompleteness and the predicate is indeterminate, rather than a body identity being minted on an unbounded claim."
  },
  "units": {
   "type": "array",
   "maxItems": 65536,
   "uniqueItems": true,
   "x-opensip-order": {
    "by": [
     "unitId"
    ]
   },
   "description": "The closed table of compilation targets. Strictly unique by unitId, and unitId is a FUNCTION of the metadata, so two contradictory rows for one purported unit are a refusal rather than two units that differ by accident. A selection or an ownership row naming a unitId with no row here is an unbound caller label and is inadmissible.",
   "items": {
    "type": "object",
    "additionalProperties": false,
    "required": [
     "unitId",
     "markerPath",
     "crateName",
     "targetKind",
     "targetName",
     "targetEdition"
    ],
    "properties": {
     "unitId": {
      "type": "string",
      "pattern": "^sha256:[0-9a-f]{64}(?![\\s\\S])",
      "x-opensip-digest": {
       "representation": "h-identity",
       "domain": "native.compilation-unit.v1",
       "form": "sha256-text",
       "retention": "derived",
       "authority": "native"
      },
      "description": "H(native.compilation-unit.v1, UnitIdentityV1{schemaVersion:1, markerPath, targetKind, targetName}) over the very fields this row carries. DERIVED retention: nothing separate is retained because the preimage IS the row, and admission re-derives and compares (sourceUnitOwnership.unitId). Not an opaque digest - preimage record, domain and selector are all published - and not a delimiter label, which could stay injective only by narrowing admitted repository paths."
     },
     "markerPath": {
      "type": "string",
      "minLength": 1,
      "maxLength": 4096,
      "pattern": "^(?!/)(?!.*(^|/)\\.\\.?(/|$))[^\\u0000\\\\]+(?![\\s\\S])",
      "description": "The inventoried manifest that declares this target: the same markerPath vocabulary WorkspaceUnitV2 already uses, cited rather than duplicated. WorkspaceUnitV2 and UnitMembershipV1 identify WORKSPACE units - cargo-package and cargo-workspace - which is the wrong granularity here, because one package holds several targets that may carry different editions; naming the marker joins the two rather than competing with them."
     },
     "crateName": {
      "type": "string",
      "minLength": 1,
      "maxLength": 256,
      "pattern": "^[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+(?![\\s\\S])",
      "description": "A key of the universe edition map, which supplies the package default."
     },
     "targetKind": {
      "type": "string",
      "enum": [
       "lib",
       "bin",
       "test",
       "bench",
       "example",
       "build-script"
      ]
     },
     "targetName": {
      "type": "string",
      "minLength": 1,
      "maxLength": 256,
      "pattern": "^[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+(?![\\s\\S])"
     },
     "targetEdition": {
      "oneOf": [
       {
        "type": "null"
       },
       {
        "type": "integer",
        "enum": [
         2015,
         2018,
         2021,
         2024
        ]
       }
      ],
      "description": "The target's OWN edition when it sets one, null when it takes the package default. Cargo documents edition on a target as well as on a package: cargo-metadata lists targets[].edition and states the package edition is a DEFAULT individual targets may differ from. null DEFERS to the universe edition map for crateName, so the two committed places can never contradict: an override is explicit and visible, and its absence is explicit too."
     }
    }
   }
  },
  "selectedUnitIds": {
   "type": "array",
   "minItems": 1,
   "maxItems": 65536,
   "uniqueItems": true,
   "x-opensip-order": "utf8",
   "items": {
    "type": "string",
    "pattern": "^sha256:[0-9a-f]{64}(?![\\s\\S])",
    "x-opensip-digest": {
     "representation": "h-identity",
     "domain": "native.compilation-unit.v1",
     "form": "sha256-text",
     "retention": "derived",
     "authority": "native"
    }
   },
   "description": "The EXPLICIT analysis scope: the compilation targets this universe analyses. Every member must be a unitId of the units table. This is what makes a shared path representable at two editions without an ambiguous claim - two selections are two universes - and it is what distinguishes SELECTED SCOPE from INCOMPLETE ENUMERATION. An owner outside the selection is deliberately out of scope and its edition does not bear on a body; an unlisted owner INSIDE the selection is a gap in knowledge and refuses. Silently omitting a conflicting owner from the units table is not a selection: the omission is invisible, whereas a selection is committed in the identity."
  },
  "ownership": {
   "type": "array",
   "maxItems": 1000000,
   "uniqueItems": true,
   "x-opensip-order": {
    "by": [
     "path",
     "unitId"
    ]
   },
   "description": "The pure relation. It carries no edition and no metadata, so it cannot contradict the units table.",
   "items": {
    "type": "object",
    "additionalProperties": false,
    "required": [
     "path",
     "unitId"
    ],
    "properties": {
     "path": {
      "type": "string",
      "minLength": 1,
      "maxLength": 4096,
      "pattern": "^(?!/)(?!.*(^|/)\\.\\.?(/|$))[^\\u0000\\\\]+(?![\\s\\S])"
     },
     "unitId": {
      "type": "string",
      "pattern": "^sha256:[0-9a-f]{64}(?![\\s\\S])",
      "x-opensip-digest": {
       "representation": "h-identity",
       "domain": "native.compilation-unit.v1",
       "form": "sha256-text",
       "retention": "derived",
       "authority": "native"
      }
     }
    }
   }
  }
 }
}
=== coop/design-corrections/native/native-evidence.schemas.v2.json#/$defs/TypeScriptConfigGraphV1
{
 "type": "object",
 "additionalProperties": false,
 "required": [
  "entryConfigPath",
  "nodes",
  "schemaVersion"
 ],
 "properties": {
  "schemaVersion": {
   "const": 1
  },
  "entryConfigPath": {
   "oneOf": [
    {
     "$ref": "#/$defs/CanonicalPath"
    },
    {
     "type": "null"
    }
   ]
  },
  "nodes": {
   "type": "array",
   "maxItems": 1024,
   "minItems": 0,
   "uniqueItems": true,
   "items": {
    "type": "object",
    "additionalProperties": false,
    "required": [
     "contentSha256",
     "extendsResolved",
     "kind",
     "path"
    ],
    "properties": {
     "path": {
      "$ref": "#/$defs/CanonicalPath"
     },
     "contentSha256": {
      "$ref": "#/$defs/DigestHex",
      "x-opensip-digest": {
       "representation": "raw-artifact",
       "artifact": "the exact inventoried snapshot bytes of that config file",
       "retention": "preimage",
       "authority": "native"
      }
     },
     "kind": {
      "type": "string",
      "enum": [
       "tsconfig",
       "jsconfig",
       "other"
      ],
      "description": "Which recognized configuration file this node is. DERIVED from node.path by the closed table at #/x-opensip-config-node-kind-law, never asserted: exact case-sensitive basename `tsconfig.json` -> tsconfig, `jsconfig.json` -> jsconfig, every other basename -> other. The rule is total and applies to EVERY node, entry and non-entry alike. `other` is an ordinary value, not a defect: `tsconfig.build.json` reached as a base is `other`, and an explicitly selected custom-named configuration is an `other` ENTRY that still derives configOrigin tsconfig. This field is inside the hashed record, so the rule is an identity rule.",
      "x-opensip-vocabulary": {
       "authority": "native/native-evidence.schemas.v2.json#/x-opensip-config-node-kind-law",
       "law": "native/native-evidence.schemas.v2.json#/x-opensip-config-node-kind-law/rule",
       "admittedBy": "native_evidence_model.typescript_config_graph_faults (native.config-graph-kind-contradicts-path), which reads the table above",
       "movesIdentity": "tsconfigGraphHash -> TypeScriptUniverseV2ResolvedInputs -> sourceUniverse -> fact2 / scope2 / coverage2 / view2 / evidence3 / seal3 / run3"
      }
     },
     "extendsResolved": {
      "type": "array",
      "maxItems": 64,
      "minItems": 0,
      "items": {
       "$ref": "#/$defs/CanonicalPath"
      },
      "x-opensip-order": "sequence",
      "description": "The ORDERED resolved extends edges of this node, in the order the config declares them. TypeScript 5.0 supports multiple entries in `extends` and a LATER entry wins, so precedence is the array order and must be retained: an unordered set or a single edge cannot represent an ordinary modern configuration. Empty means this node extends nothing. Repeated edges are retained; this is a sequence, not a set."
     }
    }
   },
   "x-opensip-order": {
    "by": [
     "path"
    ]
   }
  }
 },
 "description": "The resolved tsconfig/jsconfig extends graph a TypeScript universe commits to. entryConfigPath is the SELECTED root config the program was opened with, null exactly when the configuration was synthesized; nodes is every config file actually read, strictly ascending and unique by path, each carrying its inventoried snapshot digest, its kind and its ORDERED resolved extends edges with later-wins precedence. configOrigin is derived from the ENTRY node kind, never from the whole set: a jsconfig.json that extends a shared base.json is a jsconfig program. Every node must be reachable from the entry, every edge must name another node, and the edge graph must be acyclic. tsconfigGraphHash is the raw SHA-256 of C(this record). A selected entry whose kind is other represents an explicitly selected custom-named TypeScript configuration and derives configOrigin tsconfig. Every node's `kind` is derived from its path by the closed exact-basename table at #/x-opensip-config-node-kind-law - entry and non-entry alike - and a node whose declared kind is not the derived one refuses."
}
=== coop/design-corrections/native/native-evidence.schemas.v2.json#/$defs/ResolvedNodeModulesLayoutV1
{
 "type": "object",
 "additionalProperties": false,
 "required": [
  "entries",
  "schemaVersion"
 ],
 "properties": {
  "schemaVersion": {
   "const": 1
  },
  "entries": {
   "type": "array",
   "maxItems": 100000,
   "minItems": 0,
   "uniqueItems": true,
   "items": {
    "type": "object",
    "additionalProperties": false,
    "required": [
     "contentSha256",
     "installPath",
     "packageName",
     "packageVersion",
     "realPath"
    ],
    "properties": {
     "packageName": {
      "type": "string",
      "minLength": 1,
      "maxLength": 256
     },
     "packageVersion": {
      "type": "string",
      "minLength": 1,
      "maxLength": 256
     },
     "installPath": {
      "$ref": "#/$defs/CanonicalPath"
     },
     "realPath": {
      "$ref": "#/$defs/CanonicalPath"
     },
     "contentSha256": {
      "$ref": "#/$defs/DigestHex",
      "x-opensip-digest": {
       "representation": "raw-artifact",
       "artifact": "the exact retained bytes of that installed package manifest file; a resolution read-set observation, not a snapshot inventory row",
       "retention": "preimage",
       "authority": "native"
      }
     }
    }
   },
   "x-opensip-order": {
    "by": [
     "installPath"
    ]
   }
  }
 },
 "description": "The retained resolution read-set layout a TypeScript context commits to when nodeModulesInReadSet is true. One row per installed package DIRECTORY the resolver may read: installPath is the project-relative directory as the resolver walks it, realPath is the project-relative directory after symlink/workspace-link resolution (equal to installPath when there is no link), and contentSha256 is the raw SHA-256 of that package directory manifest file bytes (its package.json) as RETAINED, joined by digest. These rows are deliberately NOT snapshot inventory rows and must not be read as any: the one shared discovery rule prunes node_modules by path segment, so an inventory row could not exist for them, and the layout is a retained resolution observation of bytes the analysis actually read (native-evidence section 2.2, and the blobJoin -- not a snapshotJoin -- on the native.context.typescript.v2 nested record). The earlier wording said 'as inventoried in the snapshot', which contradicted both and would have led an implementer to add node_modules paths to the snapshot inventory. Rows are strictly ascending and unique by installPath, so one layout has one spelling. nodeModulesLayoutDigest is the raw SHA-256 of C(this record); it is null exactly when nodeModulesInReadSet is false."
}
=== coop/design-corrections/native/native-evidence.schemas.v2.json#/$defs/SyntaxGrammarBundleV1
{
 "type": "object",
 "additionalProperties": false,
 "description": "The installed, closure-bound grammar bundle that a syntax-only analysis parses with. It is the component that INTERPRETS a body span in that mode, so it is pinned exactly the way a toolchain is: closureId names an admitted kind=grammar closure, parserVersion must equal that closure manifest's semanticVersion (admission refuses native.syntax-grammar-version-not-from-manifest otherwise, mirroring the compiler-version rule), and bundleDigest is the exact retained bundle identity. `grammars` is the CLOSED bundled-grammar set: it is what native-evidence section 1.2's 'any file whose extension maps to a bundled grammar' actually denotes, and what makes `unsupported-file / no-bundled-grammar` decidable rather than a matter of taste.",
 "required": [
  "bundleDigest",
  "grammars",
  "normalizer",
  "parserName",
  "parserVersion",
  "schemaVersion",
  "closureId"
 ],
 "properties": {
  "schemaVersion": {
   "const": 1
  },
  "closureId": {
   "type": "string",
   "pattern": "^closure2:[0-9a-f]{64}(?![\\s\\S])"
  },
  "parserName": {
   "type": "string",
   "minLength": 1,
   "maxLength": 256,
   "description": "The component that reads the body span. Not a compiler: a syntax-only universe executes none, and naming one here would claim an interpretation that never happened."
  },
  "parserVersion": {
   "type": "string",
   "minLength": 1,
   "maxLength": 256
  },
  "bundleDigest": {
   "$ref": "#/$defs/DigestHex",
   "x-opensip-digest": {
    "representation": "raw-artifact",
    "artifact": "the exact retained bytes of the admitted grammar bundle manifest",
    "retention": "preimage",
    "authority": "native"
   }
  },
  "grammars": {
   "type": "array",
   "minItems": 1,
   "maxItems": 256,
   "uniqueItems": true,
   "x-opensip-order": {
    "by": [
     "grammarId"
    ]
   },
   "items": {
    "type": "object",
    "additionalProperties": false,
    "required": [
     "grammarDigest",
     "grammarId",
     "grammarVersion",
     "languageId",
     "suffixes",
     "syntaxClass"
    ],
    "properties": {
     "grammarId": {
      "type": "string",
      "minLength": 1,
      "maxLength": 256
     },
     "grammarVersion": {
      "type": "string",
      "minLength": 1,
      "maxLength": 256
     },
     "languageId": {
      "type": "string",
      "enum": [
       "typescript",
       "javascript",
       "rust",
       "json",
       "toml",
       "markdown",
       "yaml"
      ],
      "description": "Every language the host actually bundles a grammar for, matching BUNDLED_GRAMMARS exactly. An earlier revision listed only typescript/javascript/rust and claimed equality with the body-language-version enum as a drift check; that could not express the four data/document grammars the host has always bundled, so native-evidence section 1.2's 'any file whose extension maps to a bundled grammar' was unrepresentable for them. What is actually held equal to the body-language-version enum is the set of languages whose grammars are syntaxClass=code - see syntaxClass."
     },
     "syntaxClass": {
      "type": "string",
      "enum": [
       "code",
       "data-document"
      ],
      "description": "Which capability this grammar's files bear, and the exact distinction the original contracts already draw. `code`: the language has the body spans native-evidence section 6.3 defines - function, method, closure/lambda, impl item and block bodies - and a published L1-L3 normalisation table, so its files can bear clone body identity and the code-construct syntax relations. Its languageId MUST be a member of the foundation body-language-version languageId enum, because that identifier goes into the clone preimage. `data-document`: a bundled grammar for a data or document format with no such body spans and no published tokenisation, literalKind mapping or normalisation law anywhere in this kit. Its files are grammar-only members - accounted, never `unsupported-file` - and bear inventory evidence (file/package/vcs-change); they mint no body identity, and its languageId MUST NOT be in the body-language-version enum, since it can never appear in a clone preimage. Claiming code-construct syntax facts for a data format would require a tokenisation law this kit does not publish, which is exactly the fabricated-semantics failure the syntax-only universe exists to avoid."
     },
     "suffixes": {
      "type": "array",
      "minItems": 1,
      "maxItems": 64,
      "uniqueItems": true,
      "items": {
       "type": "string",
       "minLength": 2,
       "maxLength": 32
      },
      "x-opensip-order": "utf8"
     },
     "grammarDigest": {
      "$ref": "#/$defs/DigestHex",
      "x-opensip-digest": {
       "representation": "raw-artifact",
       "artifact": "the exact retained bytes of that grammar definition",
       "retention": "preimage",
       "authority": "native"
      }
     }
    }
   }
  },
  "normalizer": {
   "type": "object",
   "additionalProperties": false,
   "required": [
    "normalizerId",
    "normalizerVersion",
    "specificationDigest"
   ],
   "description": "The clone normalisation component this bundle ships. It is recorded so the clones rung has a bound owner in syntax-only mode; it is deliberately NOT part of the body-language-version record, because the normalisation level specification is already its own framed component of the body identity.",
   "properties": {
    "normalizerId": {
     "type": "string",
     "minLength": 1,
     "maxLength": 256
    },
    "normalizerVersion": {
     "type": "string",
     "minLength": 1,
     "maxLength": 256
    },
    "specificationDigest": {
     "$ref": "#/$defs/DigestHex",
     "x-opensip-digest": {
      "representation": "raw-artifact",
      "artifact": "the exact retained normalisation level specification bytes",
      "retention": "preimage",
      "authority": "native"
     }
    }
   }
  }
 }
}
